
insert into public.pages(slug,title,content) values
('about','About LogoColorStudio',$h$<p>LogoColorStudio is a free toolkit for designers, developers and marketers who work with brand colors every day. We started it because we were tired of opening five different websites to pull colors from a logo, check contrast, build a theme and export tokens.</p><h2>What we build</h2><p>Every tool runs in your browser wherever possible, so your uploaded logos never leave your device. Tools that need to read a public website (the Website Extractor, Website Auditor, CMS Detector and theme detectors) fetch the page on our server only to avoid browser security limits, and we do not store the results.</p><h2>Our principles</h2><ul><li><strong>Fast:</strong> results in seconds, no sign-up.</li><li><strong>Accurate:</strong> standard color math (sRGB, HSL, WCAG 2.2 contrast).</li><li><strong>Useful exports:</strong> CSS variables, Tailwind, SCSS and JSON you can paste straight into a project.</li></ul><h2>Who runs it</h2><p>LogoColorStudio is built and maintained by APWEB Ventures. Questions or ideas? Email <a href="mailto:apwebventures01@gmail.com">apwebventures01@gmail.com</a>.</p>$h$),
('contact','Contact',$h$<p>We read every message. Use the form or email us directly at <a href="mailto:apwebventures01@gmail.com">apwebventures01@gmail.com</a>. We usually reply within two business days.</p>$h$),
('privacy','Privacy Policy',$h$<p><em>Last updated: October 2026</em></p><p>This Privacy Policy explains how LogoColorStudio ("we", "us") collects, uses and protects information when you use our website.</p><h2>Information we collect</h2><ul><li><strong>Uploaded images:</strong> logos you upload to color tools are processed locally in your browser and are not uploaded to our servers.</li><li><strong>URLs you submit:</strong> when you use URL-based tools, we fetch that public page to analyse it. We keep a short-lived record of your IP address only for rate limiting.</li><li><strong>Contact and newsletter:</strong> if you send a message or subscribe, we store your name, email and message.</li><li><strong>Usage data:</strong> anonymous page and tool usage counts, and a random visitor ID stored in your browser to count palette likes.</li></ul><h2>Cookies and advertising</h2><p>We use cookies and similar technologies for essential functions, remembering your theme and consent choices, and, with your consent, for advertising.</p><p>We use <strong>Google AdSense</strong> to show ads. Third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this and other websites. Google's use of advertising cookies, including the <strong>DoubleClick cookie</strong>, enables it and its partners to serve ads based on your visits to our site and/or other sites on the Internet.</p><p>You may opt out of personalised advertising by visiting <a href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Ads Settings</a>. You can also opt out of third-party vendors' use of cookies for personalised advertising at <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">www.aboutads.info</a> and, in the EU, <a href="https://www.youronlinechoices.eu/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">youronlinechoices.eu</a>. Learn more in <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads" rel="noopener" target="_blank">How Google uses data</a>.</p><h2>Your choices</h2><p>You can accept, reject or manage non-essential cookies through our cookie banner at any time using the "Cookie settings" link in the footer. Under GDPR and CCPA you may request access to, correction of or deletion of your personal data by emailing us.</p><h2>Data retention</h2><p>Contact messages and subscriber emails are kept until you ask us to delete them. Rate-limit records are deleted automatically.</p><h2>Children</h2><p>Our service is not directed to children under 13.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>Email <a href="mailto:apwebventures01@gmail.com">apwebventures01@gmail.com</a> with any privacy request.</p>$h$),
('terms','Terms of Service',$h$<p><em>Last updated: October 2026</em></p><p>By using LogoColorStudio you agree to these terms.</p><h2>Use of the service</h2><p>Our tools are provided free of charge for personal and commercial use. You must not use them to break the law, overload our servers, or scan websites you are not permitted to analyse.</p><h2>Your content</h2><p>You keep all rights to logos and content you upload. You confirm you have the right to use them.</p><h2>Results</h2><p>Color values, audits and detections are generated automatically and may contain mistakes. Always verify important results.</p><h2>Third-party sites</h2><p>We are not responsible for the content of websites you analyse or that we link to.</p><h2>Liability</h2><p>The service is provided "as is" without warranties. To the extent permitted by law, we are not liable for any damages arising from its use.</p><h2>Changes</h2><p>We may update these terms. Continued use means you accept the updated terms.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p><a href="mailto:apwebventures01@gmail.com">apwebventures01@gmail.com</a></p>$h$),
('cookies','Cookie Policy',$h$<p><em>Last updated: October 2026</em></p><p>Cookies are small text files stored on your device. We also use browser local storage, which works similarly.</p><h2>Cookies we use</h2><ul><li><strong>Essential:</strong> your cookie consent choice, theme (light or dark), and admin sign-in session.</li><li><strong>Functional:</strong> a random visitor ID used so each visitor can like a palette once, and your saved favourite palettes.</li><li><strong>Advertising (only with consent):</strong> Google AdSense and its partners, including the DoubleClick cookie, to show and measure ads.</li></ul><h2>Managing cookies</h2><p>Use the "Cookie settings" link in the footer to change your choice at any time. You can also delete cookies in your browser settings. To opt out of personalised ads visit <a href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Ads Settings</a> or <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">aboutads.info</a>.</p>$h$),
('disclaimer','Disclaimer',$h$<p>The information and tools on LogoColorStudio are provided for general informational purposes only. While we aim for accuracy, we make no guarantees about the completeness or reliability of any color value, audit score or detection result.</p><p>Website audits, CMS and theme detections are based on publicly visible signals and may be incomplete or wrong. They are not professional SEO, legal or security advice.</p><p>Brand names and trademarks mentioned (such as WordPress, Shopify or Google) belong to their owners. We are not affiliated with them.</p><p>Some pages display advertising. Ads are clearly labelled "Advertisement".</p>$h$);

insert into public.blog_posts(slug,title,excerpt,cover_image,category,tags,status,published_at,seo_title,meta_description,content) values
('how-to-extract-colors-from-a-logo','How to Extract Colors from a Logo (Step-by-Step)','Pull exact HEX, RGB and HSL values from any logo file and turn them into a usable brand palette.','/images/blog/cover-1.webp','Guides','{colors,branding,logo}','published','2026-09-02','How to Extract Colors from a Logo','Learn how to pull exact HEX, RGB and HSL colors from any logo and turn them into a brand palette you can use in CSS and Tailwind.',
$h$<p>Every brand starts with a few colors. If all you have is a logo file, getting those colors right is the first job before you design a website, slide deck or social post. Here is a reliable method that works for PNG, JPG, SVG and WebP logos.</p><h2>1. Start with the best file you have</h2><p>Use the largest, cleanest version of the logo. Compressed JPGs add noise around edges, creating dozens of "in-between" shades that are not real brand colors. A transparent PNG or SVG gives the cleanest results.</p><h2>2. Remove backgrounds and anti-aliasing</h2><p>Edges of shapes are blended with the background. Good extractors ignore transparent and near-transparent pixels and group similar shades together, so you get the true brand color rather than the blurry edge.</p><h2>3. Group similar pixels</h2><p>Color extraction tools use clustering: they place every pixel into a bucket of similar colors, then return the average of the biggest buckets. In the <a href="/tools/logo-extractor">Logo Extractor</a>, you can choose how many colors to return and see what share of the logo each one covers.</p><h2>4. Check the values</h2><ul><li><strong>HEX</strong> (#0A8CFF) is the standard for web and design tools.</li><li><strong>RGB</strong> (10, 140, 255) is useful in code and video software.</li><li><strong>HSL</strong> (208°, 100%, 52%) makes it easy to create lighter and darker variants.</li></ul><h2>5. Build a full palette</h2><p>A logo usually has two to four colors, but a website needs neutrals, backgrounds, borders and states. Use the <a href="/palettes">Palette Generator</a> to create complementary, analogous and monochromatic variations from your logo colors.</p><h2>6. Test contrast</h2><p>Brand colors are not always readable as text. Check each foreground/background pair with the <a href="/tools/contrast-checker">Contrast Checker</a> and aim for WCAG AA (4.5:1) for body text.</p><h2>7. Export tokens</h2><p>Finally, export your palette as CSS variables, Tailwind config or JSON from the <a href="/tools/export-center">Export Center</a> so developers use the exact same values everywhere.</p><h2>Summary</h2><p>Clean file, ignore edges, cluster, verify, extend, test, export. Follow these seven steps and your brand colors will look identical in every place they appear.</p>$h$),
('color-psychology-in-branding','Color Psychology in Branding: What Each Color Says','How colors shape first impressions, and how to choose a brand palette that matches your message.','/images/blog/cover-2.webp','Branding','{branding,psychology}','published','2026-09-10','Color Psychology in Branding Explained','What blue, red, green, orange and other colors communicate in branding, plus practical tips for choosing a palette that fits your audience.',
$h$<p>People form an opinion about a brand within seconds, and color carries much of that first impression. Color psychology is not a strict rulebook, since culture and context matter, but some associations are consistent enough to guide your choices.</p><h2>Blue: trust and calm</h2><p>Blue is the most common color in finance, technology and healthcare because it suggests reliability and stability. Brighter blues feel energetic and modern; navy feels established and serious.</p><h2>Red: energy and urgency</h2><p>Red attracts attention quickly. It is popular for food, entertainment and sales messages. Use it for accents and calls to action rather than large backgrounds, which can feel aggressive.</p><h2>Green: growth and health</h2><p>Green connects to nature, money and wellbeing. Fresh greens suit eco and wellness brands, while deep greens feel premium.</p><h2>Orange and yellow: friendliness and optimism</h2><p>Warm, cheerful colors feel approachable and creative. They work well for brands targeting younger audiences, but yellow text often fails contrast checks, so pair it with dark text.</p><h2>Purple: creativity and luxury</h2><p>Historically linked to royalty, purple reads as imaginative and premium.</p><h2>Black, white and gray</h2><p>Neutrals give structure. Black suggests sophistication; white suggests simplicity and space.</p><h2>Combining colors</h2><p>Most strong brands use one dominant color, one or two supporting colors and a set of neutrals. A popular guideline is 60% neutral, 30% primary, 10% accent. Our <a href="/palettes">palette gallery</a> has over 1,000 ready-made combinations for inspiration.</p><h2>Test with your audience</h2><p>Associations vary by country and industry. Show palette options to real customers, and always check readability with the <a href="/tools/contrast-checker">Contrast Checker</a>.</p>$h$),
('wcag-contrast-guide','WCAG Contrast Guide: AA vs AAA Made Simple','Understand contrast ratios, the WCAG AA and AAA levels, and how to fix colors that fail.','/images/blog/cover-3.webp','Accessibility','{accessibility,wcag,contrast}','published','2026-09-18','WCAG Contrast Guide: AA vs AAA','A plain-English guide to WCAG contrast ratios, AA and AAA requirements for text and UI, and quick ways to fix failing color pairs.',
$h$<p>Roughly one in twelve men and one in two hundred women have some form of color vision deficiency, and many more people read screens in bright sunlight. Good contrast helps everyone.</p><h2>What is a contrast ratio?</h2><p>The contrast ratio compares the relative luminance of two colors, from 1:1 (identical) to 21:1 (black on white). WCAG uses this number to set minimum levels.</p><h2>The requirements</h2><ul><li><strong>AA normal text:</strong> at least 4.5:1</li><li><strong>AA large text</strong> (24px, or 19px bold): at least 3:1</li><li><strong>AAA normal text:</strong> at least 7:1</li><li><strong>AAA large text:</strong> at least 4.5:1</li><li><strong>UI components and icons:</strong> at least 3:1 against neighbours</li></ul><h2>How to check</h2><p>Paste your foreground and background colors into the <a href="/tools/contrast-checker">Contrast Checker</a>. It shows the ratio and a pass or fail for each level instantly.</p><h2>How to fix a failing pair</h2><ol><li>Darken the text or lighten the background (adjust lightness in HSL and keep the hue).</li><li>Increase font size or weight so the large-text threshold applies.</li><li>Use your brand color for backgrounds and buttons, with white or near-black text on top.</li></ol><h2>Do not rely on color alone</h2><p>Links, errors and charts should also use underlines, icons or labels so the meaning is clear without color.</p><p>Build an accessible light and dark theme automatically with the <a href="/tools/theme-generator">Theme Generator</a>.</p>$h$),
('how-to-detect-a-wordpress-theme','How to Detect a WordPress Theme Any Site Uses','Find the theme name, version, author and plugins behind any WordPress website, manually or in one click.','/images/blog/cover-4.webp','Tutorials','{wordpress,themes}','published','2026-09-26','How to Detect a WordPress Theme','Find out which WordPress theme and plugins a website uses: manual source-code method and a one-click detector, plus what to do with the result.',
$h$<p>Seen a WordPress site you love and want to know which theme it uses? WordPress leaves clear clues in the page source. Here is how to find them.</p><h2>Method 1: the one-click way</h2><p>Enter the address into our <a href="/tools/wordpress-theme-detector">WordPress Theme Detector</a>. It reads the page, finds the active theme folder, opens the theme's <code>style.css</code> and shows the name, version, author, theme URI, parent theme and detected plugins.</p><h2>Method 2: view the source</h2><ol><li>Open the site, right-click and choose <em>View Page Source</em>.</li><li>Search for <code>/wp-content/themes/</code>. The folder name after it is the theme slug, for example <code>/wp-content/themes/astra/</code>.</li><li>Open <code>https://example.com/wp-content/themes/astra/style.css</code>. The comment at the top lists Theme Name, Author, Version and, for child themes, a Template line naming the parent.</li><li>Search for <code>/wp-content/plugins/</code> to see plugin folders.</li></ol><h2>Why the result can be hidden</h2><ul><li>Custom or renamed themes show unfamiliar names.</li><li>Caching and optimisation plugins can combine files and hide paths.</li><li>Some sites are headless WordPress with a different front end.</li></ul><h2>Is it WordPress at all?</h2><p>If you find no <code>wp-content</code> paths, use the <a href="/tools/cms-detector">CMS Detector</a> to check for Shopify, Wix, Webflow and others.</p><h2>Respect the creator</h2><p>Use detection for inspiration and research. Buy themes from their official sources and never copy a site's custom design or content.</p>$h$),
('best-color-palettes-for-websites','Best Color Palettes for Websites in 2026','Twelve proven palette styles for modern websites, with tips on when to use each.','/images/blog/cover-5.webp','Inspiration','{palettes,web design}','published','2026-10-04','Best Color Palettes for Websites (2026)','Discover the best website color palette styles for 2026, from calm neutrals to bold neon, with practical tips to apply them in your design.',
$h$<p>The right palette makes a website feel coherent and trustworthy. These are the palette styles that work best right now, and how to use them.</p><h2>1. Soft neutral with one bold accent</h2><p>Warm off-whites, stone grays and a single vivid accent for buttons. Clean and versatile for SaaS and portfolios.</p><h2>2. Deep navy and electric blue</h2><p>Dark backgrounds with bright blue highlights feel technical and premium. Ideal for developer tools.</p><h2>3. Earth tones</h2><p>Terracotta, olive, sand and clay suit sustainable brands, food and lifestyle sites.</p><h2>4. Pastel gradients</h2><p>Soft peach, lilac and mint gradients feel friendly. Pair with dark text for contrast.</p><h2>5. Monochrome blue or green</h2><p>Several shades of one hue look calm and organised and are easy to keep accessible.</p><h2>6. Retro warm</h2><p>Mustard, burnt orange and teal bring a 70s feel to creative brands.</p><h2>7. Neon on black</h2><p>High-energy palettes for gaming, music and events. Keep neon to accents.</p><h2>8. Luxury dark</h2><p>Charcoal, black and gold or champagne for fashion and premium products.</p><h2>9. Fresh vibrant</h2><p>Bright, saturated complementary colors for playful consumer apps.</p><h2>10. Cool corporate</h2><p>Slate, steel blue and white for finance and consulting.</p><h2>11. Nature greens</h2><p>Forest, sage and leaf green for wellness and outdoor brands.</p><h2>12. Your logo palette</h2><p>Often the best palette is already in your logo. Upload it to the <a href="/tools/logo-extractor">Logo Extractor</a> and generate harmonies automatically.</p><h2>Find more</h2><p>Browse and like over 1,000 curated palettes in our <a href="/palettes">palette gallery</a>, filter by tags like Pastel, Dark or Earth, and export any of them in one click.</p>$h$);
